dzn: Take flat inputs from the last vertex without barycentrics

dzn-provoking-vertex-last.patch reads flat inputs at the last vertex with
attributeAtVertex, which needs barycentrics support in Direct3D 12. Intel's
drivers under WSL2 have none (a UHD 770 and an Iris Plus 650), so there
every flat varying and integer fragment output of an OpenGL ES app under
ANGLE still came from the first vertex (dEQP-GLES3 fragment_out int/uint
and rasterization.flatshading: 555 failures on a UHD 770).

There, a pipeline with VK_PROVOKING_VERTEX_MODE_LAST_VERTEX_EXT, a vertex
shader as its last geometry stage, a line or triangle topology and a
fragment shader with flat inputs gets a geometry shader that passes each
primitive through, the vertex order unchanged, with the flat outputs of
its last vertex in every vertex. Which vertex that is depends on the
topology, which ANGLE sets dynamically, so it comes from a new field of the
runtime data, flat_vertex, set at each draw: the third vertex of a list
triangle or of a triangle of Direct3D 12's own fans, the second of a line
and of the (i + 1, i + 2, 0) triangles Dozen draws a fan as, and for a
triangle strip the third of an even triangle and the second of an odd
one, which Direct3D 12 hands over as (n, n + 2, n + 1). Topologies with
adjacency, which OpenGL ES only draws through a geometry shader of its
own, keep the first vertex, as does a fragment shader that reads the
primitive ID.

Whether the geometry shader takes lines or triangles goes into the
pipeline hash, which has no topology of its own: without it a lines
pipeline got the cached geometry shader of a triangles pipeline with the
same shaders, and drew wrong flat values (dEQP-GLES3
transform_feedback.basic_types.interleaved lines and triangles with
lowp and mediump uvec4, which reuse one program).

dEQP-VK.rasterization.provoking_vertex.draw: 28 of 36 pass on a UHD 770
and an Iris Plus 650, up from 18; the 8 left are the adjacency topologies.

